<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarraco</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tarraco was a Roman city on the coast of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the predecessor of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a>. Founded as a military base by the Scipio brothers in 218 BCE during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, it became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a> and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> Tarraconensis, the largest Roman province in Spain. Its amphitheatre, walls and other remains are a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarraco.jpg" alt="Tarraco" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rafa Esteve · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarragona_-_Amfiteatre_04_2016-08-31.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was inhabited from the 5th century BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>, who had commercial contacts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> settled on the coast. Tarraco is first mentioned after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-calvus">Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Calvus</a> arrived at Empúries in 218 BCE at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, which began the Roman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. In 217 BCE reinforcements arrived under Publius Scipio, and the two brothers are credited with strengthening the fortifications and establishing a military port.</p><p>The Roman wall was probably built on top of an older Iberian one. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> used Tarraco as his winter base in 211 and 210 BCE, and the fishermen of the city served with their boats at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>. The conquest of the peninsula took over 200 years, and Tarraco stayed a supply and winter base in the wars against the Celtiberians. Strabo says the governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a> also resided there.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> probably made it a colonia around 45 BCE, as Colonia Iulia Urbs Triumphalis Tarraco. In 27 BCE Augustus replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a> with the larger province of Hispania Tarraconensis, named for its capital, and spent time at Tarraco while directing the campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>. The old Via Herculea became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-augusta">Via Augusta</a>, and the city flourished, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomponius-mela">Pomponius Mela</a> calling it the richest port on that coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Galba</a> lived there for eight years before being proclaimed emperor in 68 CE.</p><p>The amphitheatre and the provincial forum were probably built after Vespasian's reorganisation of the province, and Hadrian is thought to have held a conventus there in the winter of 122 to 123. Economic difficulties set in at the end of the 2nd century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s reforms left Tarraco the capital of a much smaller province. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euric">Euric</a> occupied it in 476, apparently without much destruction, and the Muslims arrived in 713 or 714, ending its ancient history.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tarraco is the ancient name of the present city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a> in Catalonia, and the oldest Roman settlement on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. Its archaeological ensemble is one of the largest sites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Roman Hispania</a> preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared it a World Heritage Site in 2000, citing its importance in Roman planning and urban design and its testimony to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> world in antiquity.</p><p>The amphitheatre stands near the shore with large parts of its structure surviving. The Roman aqueduct of Les Ferreres, called the Devil's Bridge, crosses a valley about 4 kilometres from the city, and the Tower of the Scipios, a Roman tomb, lies 1.5 kilometres to the north-west.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tarraco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frankish bands crossed into Gaul by land and raided by sea along the coasts of Gaul and Britain, and some are said to have reached Spain and sacked <b>Tarraco</b> in the 260s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When the people of <b>Tarraco</b> told Augustus that a palm tree had sprouted on his altar, he reportedly replied that this showed how often they lit fires on it, according to Quintilian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Kese, the centre of the Cessetani, became <b>Tarraco</b>, the capital of Hispania Tarraconensis; Arse became Saguntum, Iltrida Ilerda, Laieta Barcino, now Barcelona, and Salduie, in the land of the Sedetani, Caesaraugusta, now Zaragoza.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 218 BCE Gnaeus Scipio defeated the Carthaginian commander Hanno near Cissa, a town sometimes identified with Kese, and the Romans established their base at <b>Tarraco</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cessetani">Cessetani</a></li><li><blockquote>It grew from Rome's wartime base at <b>Tarraco</b>, established after Gnaeus Scipio's landing in 218 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a></li></ul></section>
